\chapter*{Abstract}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}

This report presents the design, implementation and validation of a low-cost embedded battery monitoring and protection demonstration platform for a 3S lithium-ion battery pack. The project addresses the need for an observable and testable BMS structure that can demonstrate core monitoring and protection principles without the complexity of an industrial-grade system. The platform is based on an STM32 NUCLEO-G491RE controller and integrates an INA228 digital power monitor for voltage and current measurement, an NTC thermistor circuit for temperature sensing, an SSD1306 OLED display, MOSFET-based load control, simplified State-of-Charge indication and Flash-based runtime statistics. The firmware uses a bare-metal superloop with timed sensor acquisition, button handling, display update, protection logic and data retention. Subsystem tests verified the voltage/current measurement, temperature conversion, OLED interface, MOSFET switching, SoC display and Flash statistics functions. The integrated system test showed that the platform could acquire battery parameters, display system status, respond to user input, detect abnormal temperature/current/load conditions and disconnect the external load through the MOSFET stage. The results confirm that the system is suitable for educational demonstration and prototype-level validation. However, it remains limited by short-duration laboratory testing and does not include cell balancing, charging control, certified safety validation, long-term reliability testing or advanced SoC/SoH estimation.

