\chapter{Introduction}

\section{Background and Motivation}

Lithium-ion battery systems are now widely used in electric vehicles, portable electronics, and stationary energy storage systems. As these applications continue to expand, reliable battery monitoring and protection have become increasingly important [1], [3]. In a battery system, voltage, current, and temperature are the most fundamental variables because they directly indicate the electrical and thermal condition of the battery. These parameters also provide the basis for abnormal-condition detection, load control, and basic state evaluation [1], [2].

A Battery Management System (BMS) is therefore a key part of a lithium-ion battery system. Its role is not only to measure battery parameters, but also to support safe and understandable system operation. However, many existing BMS products and research systems are highly integrated, complex, and application-specific [2]–[4]. Although this is suitable for commercial and industrial use, it can make the internal working process difficult to observe. For teaching, demonstration, and early-stage validation, such systems may behave like a “black box”, where students can see the input and output but cannot easily understand how sensing, decision-making, display, and protection are connected.

The main motivation of this project is therefore to develop a low-cost and transparent battery monitoring and protection demonstration platform. Instead of aiming to reproduce a full industrial BMS, the project focuses on the essential functions needed to show how a basic BMS operates. These include voltage and current measurement, temperature monitoring, real-time status display, and MOSFET-based load protection. By keeping the hardware structure clear and the software behaviour observable, the system is intended to support educational use, classroom demonstration, and prototype-level validation.

This project does not aim to achieve industrial-grade accuracy or complete commercial functionality. Instead, it aims to provide a clear, testable, and extendable embedded BMS demonstration platform. This direction allows the project to balance cost, functionality, and implementation simplicity while still addressing the core monitoring and protection requirements of lithium-ion battery systems.

\section{Aim and Objectives}

The overall aim of this project is to design, implement, and validate a low-cost embedded battery monitoring and protection demonstration platform for lithium-ion battery systems. The platform is intended to show the essential operating principles of a basic BMS in a clear and observable way, with emphasis on real-time measurement, status display, and protection control [2], [3]. Rather than pursuing the full complexity of an industrial BMS, the project focuses on the core functions required to make battery condition monitoring and abnormal-condition response understandable, testable, and suitable for educational demonstration.

To achieve this aim, the following objectives are defined:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item To design and implement voltage and current measurement functions for monitoring the electrical condition of the battery system.
    \item To design and implement temperature sensing based on an NTC thermistor for observing the thermal condition of the system.
    \item To integrate voltage, current, and temperature acquisition into a real-time embedded monitoring framework.
    \item To implement basic protection logic for abnormal conditions, including over-temperature, over-current, low-current or load-related abnormal behaviour, and low-SoC conditions.
    \item To implement MOSFET-based load switching so that the monitoring results can be linked to a physical protection action.
    \item To develop an OLED-based display interface for presenting key battery parameters, warning states, and system status in real time.
    \item To include supporting demonstration functions, such as simplified SoC estimation and Flash-based runtime statistics, to improve the completeness and observability of the platform.
\end{enumerate}

Each objective is intended to be verified through subsystem testing and overall system validation. This allows the final work to be assessed not only by whether the individual functions are implemented, but also by whether the integrated platform can demonstrate a coherent battery monitoring and protection process.

\section{Project Scope}

The scope of this project is limited to the design, implementation, and validation of a low-cost embedded BMS demonstration platform for a 3S lithium-ion battery pack. The platform is centred on an STM32 microcontroller and includes the main hardware and software elements required to demonstrate basic battery monitoring and protection. The hardware scope includes the STM32 NUCLEO-G491RE board, INA228 voltage and current sensing module, NTC thermistor temperature sensing circuit, OLED display, MOSFET-based load control circuit, custom PCB design, battery pack connection, external load, laser-cut mounting base, and 3D-printed battery cover.

The functional scope focuses on the acquisition of three core battery parameters: voltage, current, and temperature. Based on these measurements, the system provides real-time status display, basic abnormal-condition detection, and MOSFET-based protection action. Supporting functions, including simplified SoC estimation and Flash-based runtime statistics, are also included to improve the completeness and observability of the demonstration platform. These functions are treated as supporting features rather than the main technical focus of the project.

The project also includes embedded software development, sensor interface implementation, user interface design, protection logic, PCB-level integration, mechanical arrangement, subsystem testing, and overall system validation. The main purpose of the validation is to confirm that the platform can measure key parameters, display system status, detect abnormal conditions, and execute a physical protection response.

This project does not aim to produce a complete industrial-grade BMS. It does not include cell balancing, charging control, advanced SoC or SoH estimation algorithms, certification testing, long-term reliability assessment, or product-level industrial deployment. These excluded functions are commonly associated with more complete commercial or industrial BMS designs [4], [8], [11], [20].

