\chapter{Literature Review}

\section{Battery Management Systems and Their Core Functions}

A battery management system (BMS) is generally used to ensure that a lithium-ion battery system operates within acceptable electrical and thermal limits. In the literature, the main functions of a BMS normally include parameter monitoring, fault protection, state estimation, cell balancing, thermal management, and communication with external systems [2], [3], [13]. Among these functions, monitoring and protection are the most fundamental because other higher-level functions depend on reliable information about the battery condition.

The most important monitored variables are voltage, current, and temperature. Voltage is directly related to over-charge and over-discharge detection, current reflects charge and discharge behaviour, and temperature indicates thermal stress and potential safety risk [1], [3]. These parameters provide the basic information required for a BMS to judge whether the battery is operating normally. If abnormal conditions such as over-temperature, ovr-current, or unsafe voltage behaviour are detected, the BMS should respond through warning, control, or protection actions. Therefore, the basic functional chain of a BMS can be described as measurement, judgement, protection, and status reporting.

Over the last decade, BMS development has moved beyond simple protection circuits towards more integrated and intelligent systems. Recent reviews show that modern BMS research increasingly focuses on high-precision sensing, digital implementation, data-driven state estimation, functional safety, and system-level integration [1], [2], [9], [19]. Hardware architectures have also become more specialised, with different concepts used for distributed, centralised, and modular battery systems [4]. These developments are important for electric vehicles and large-scale energy storage systems, where reliability, scalability, and safety requirements are high.

However, a complete industrial BMS can be complex. Functions such as cell balancing, charging control, advanced SoC and SoH estimation, thermal management systems, and standard-compliant safety validation often require additional hardware, more complex algorithms, and longer testing procedures [8], [11], [12], [20]. Although these functions are important in commercial applications, they are not always necessary for a low-cost educational demonstration platform. For such a platform, the priority is to make the basic operation of a BMS clear and experimentally verifiable.

Therefore, this project focuses on the core BMS functions that are most suitable for demonstration: voltage, current, and temperature monitoring, abnormal-condition judgement, MOSFET-based protection control, and real-time status display. Supporting functions such as simplified SoC representation and runtime statistics can improve system completeness, but they are not treated as the main technical focus. This approach follows the general trend of BMS development while keeping the system structure transparent, low-cost, and suitable for teaching and validation purposes.


\section{Voltage, Current and Temperature Monitoring}
Voltage, current, and temperature are the most important low-level measurements in lithium-ion battery monitoring. Voltage indicates the electrical operating range of the battery and is closely related to over-charge and over-discharge protection. It can also provide useful information for state estimation when the battery is close to equilibrium. However, terminal voltage alone is not sufficient to describe the real battery condition, because it is affected by load current, internal resistance, temperature, and relaxation behaviour [14], [16]. Therefore, voltage monitoring is necessary, but it should not be used as the only indicator of battery state.

Current measurement provides information about charge and discharge behaviour. It is important for over-current protection, power calculation, energy estimation, and Coulomb-counting-based SoC estimation [14], [17]. In practical BMS designs, current can be measured using shunt-resistor sensing, Hall-effect sensors, or integrated current and power monitoring devices. Hall-effect sensors provide electrical isolation and are suitable for higher-current systems, but they can increase cost and size. Shunt-based sensing is simpler and cheaper, but it requires careful voltage measurement across a small resistance. Integrated digital power monitors combine shunt-voltage and bus-voltage measurement with digital communication, which can reduce analogue front-end complexity in embedded systems [1], [5].

Temperature monitoring is equally important because lithium-ion batteries are sensitive to thermal stress. Excessive temperature can accelerate ageing, reduce reliability, and increase safety risk, especially under high-current or fault conditions [12], [21]. Common temperature sensing methods include thermocouples, platinum resistance temperature detectors such as Pt100 or Pt1000, and NTC thermistors. Thermocouples can cover a wide temperature range, but they normally require cold-junction compensation and additional signal conditioning. Platinum RTDs offer good accuracy and stability, but their cost and interface complexity may be unnecessarily high for a low-cost demonstration platform. NTC thermistors are cheaper, compact, and easy to attach near the battery or load path, making them suitable for basic thermal monitoring where extreme precision is not required.

For this project, voltage, current, and temperature are selected as the core monitored parameters because they provide complementary information. Voltage shows the electrical operating range, current shows the dynamic load condition, and temperature shows the thermal condition of the system. Using these three measurements together allows the platform to demonstrate a more complete monitoring process than a voltage-only design. This supports the development of a low-cost BMS demonstration platform that can show not only measured values, but also the relationship between sensing, abnormal-condition judgement, and protection action.

\section{State-of-Charge Estimation in Low-Cost Systems}
State-of-Charge (SoC) estimation is an important function in battery management because it provides an indication of the remaining available charge in a battery system. Unlike voltage, current, or temperature, SoC cannot be measured directly by a single sensor. It must be estimated from measurable quantities such as terminal voltage, current, temperature, and previous operating history [14], [15]. In practical BMS applications, SoC information is useful for user display, energy management, low-charge warning, and protection-related decision making [2], [3], [13].

Several SoC estimation methods are commonly discussed in the literature. The open-circuit voltage (OCV) method is simple and low-cost, but it is most reliable when the battery is close to equilibrium. Under load conditions, terminal voltage is affected by internal resistance, polarisation, hysteresis, and temperature, which limits the accuracy of a voltage-only SoC estimate [16], [25]. Coulomb counting estimates SoC by integrating current over time and is suitable for real-time tracking, but it can accumulate drift due to current measurement error, initial SoC uncertainty, and capacity variation [17], [18].

More advanced methods, such as Kalman-filter-based observers, equivalent-circuit-model-based estimation, and data-driven or AI-based methods, can improve estimation accuracy under dynamic operating conditions [14], [17], [18]. However, these methods normally require accurate model parameters, calibration data, battery characterisation tests, or a larger amount of training data. This increases the development complexity and makes them less suitable for a low-cost demonstration platform where transparency, implementation simplicity, and real-time operation are more important than high-precision battery modelling.

For this project, SoC estimation is included mainly to make the battery state more understandable to the user. A lightweight hybrid approach is therefore more appropriate than a full advanced estimator. The implemented method combines OCV-based initialisation, current integration through Coulomb counting, and a simple voltage-based correction or filtering stage. This allows the OLED interface to present a reasonable indication of battery state while keeping the algorithm understandable and suitable for embedded implementation. The SoC result is therefore used as a supporting display and warning indicator rather than as a highly calibrated measurement output.

\section{Existing Low-Cost and Prototype BMS Solutions}
Low-cost and prototype-level BMS solutions are widely discussed in applications such as photovoltaic systems, residential energy storage, IoT-based monitoring, and compact battery monitoring tools [10], [22]–[24]. These studies show that low-cost platforms can be useful for early validation, data acquisition, and small-scale energy storage applications. Compared with industrial BMS products, they usually reduce hardware cost and implementation complexity by using embedded controllers, simpler sensing circuits, and limited protection functions.

However, many low-cost or prototype systems still have limitations from a demonstration perspective. Some systems focus mainly on data collection or remote monitoring, while the internal relationship between sensing, decision-making, protection action, and user feedback is not always clearly visible [22], [23]. In this sense, the system may still behave like a black box: measured data are produced and outputs are shown, but the complete operating chain is not easy to observe or explain. This limits their value in teaching environments where the purpose is not only to obtain results, but also to understand how the BMS works.

Another limitation is functional separation. A low-cost monitoring platform may measure voltage, current, or temperature, but may not clearly connect these measurements to real-time display and physical protection control. Conversely, more complete BMS architectures may include balancing, advanced estimation, communication, and safety validation, but this increases hardware and software complexity [2]–[4], [8], [11]. For educational and validation-oriented systems, a balance is therefore needed between functional completeness, transparency, cost, and implementation effort.

This project addresses this gap by focusing on a low-cost BMS demonstration platform rather than a commercial BMS replacement. The intended contribution is not to outperform industrial systems, but to provide a clear and observable platform in which voltage, current, and temperature monitoring are connected to display, abnormal-condition judgement, and MOSFET-based protection. This makes the system more suitable for explaining the basic BMS operating chain and for validating the feasibility of a compact embedded implementation.

\section{Design Implications for This Project}
The literature review shows that a BMS must first provide reliable monitoring and protection before more advanced functions can be meaningful. Although modern BMS development is moving towards higher integration, more advanced sensing, and more intelligent state estimation, the most essential requirement remains the ability to measure key battery parameters and respond to abnormal conditions [1]–[3], [13]. For this project, this means that voltage, current, and temperature monitoring should form the technical foundation of the system.

A second implication is that the design must remain transparent and suitable for demonstration. Many complete BMS architectures include functions such as balancing, charging control, communication, and advanced estimation, but these functions also increase system complexity [4], [8], [11], [14]. For an educational demonstration platform, excessive complexity can make the system harder to understand and less effective for explaining the basic BMS operating process. Therefore, the design should prioritise a clear measurement-to-protection chain rather than the maximum number of functions.

A third implication is the need to balance reliability, low cost, and implementation simplicity. Low-cost BMS and monitoring platforms reported in the literature show that embedded controllers and compact sensing circuits can support practical validation, but their value depends on how clearly the measured data are connected to system behaviour [10], [22]–[24]. This supports the selection of an STM32-based embedded controller, an integrated digital voltage and current monitoring device, an NTC thermistor for temperature sensing, an OLED for local status display, and a MOSFET for visible load-control action.

Therefore, the design direction of this project is not to develop the most advanced estimation algorithm or a full industrial BMS. Instead, the project aims to implement a clear and verifiable BMS demonstration platform in which sensing, judgement, display, and protection can be observed as one connected process. Based on these design implications, the next chapter presents the overall system architecture and explains how the selected hardware and software structure supports this objective.

