\section{Overall System Architecture}

\begingroup
\setstretch{1.15}
Figure~3.2.1 shows the overall physical arrangement of the developed low-cost BMS demonstration platform. The system is built around a 3S lithium-ion battery pack, a NUCLEO-G491RE development board, an INA228 voltage and current sensing module, an NTC temperature sensing circuit, an OLED display, a MOSFET load-switching stage, and an external load. The NUCLEO-G491RE is suitable for this type of embedded demonstration because it provides an STM32G491RET6 microcontroller, access to STM32G4 I/O pins through expansion headers, and an integrated STLINK-V3E debugger/programmer for development and testing~\cite{ref6}.

The overall architecture can be divided into two main paths. The first is the power path, where the battery pack supplies the load through the current sensing and MOSFET switching stage. This allows the platform to demonstrate how the load can be disconnected when abnormal operation is detected. The second is the signal and control path, where the INA228 provides voltage and current information to the STM32 through I2C, while the NTC circuit provides temperature information through an ADC input. The STM32 then processes these measurements, updates the OLED display, and controls the MOSFET according to the protection logic.

The system also includes supporting software functions such as simplified SoC indication and Flash-based runtime statistics. These functions improve the completeness of the platform but remain secondary to the main monitoring and protection chain. Overall, the architecture follows a clear sequence of sensing, processing, display, and protection control, which provides the basis for the more detailed hardware and software descriptions in the following sections.

