\section{Hardware and Software Architecture}




Figure~\ref{fig:hardware_architecture} presents the hardware architecture of the developed BMS demonstration platform. The system is arranged around a 3S lithium-ion battery pack, which supplies the external load through the INA228 high-side measurement path and the MOSFET-based load-switching stage. The STM32 microcontroller reads voltage, current, and temperature data, updates the OLED display, and drives the MOSFET so that a software protection decision can be converted into a physical load-disconnection action.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{figures/hardware_architecture.png}
\caption{Hardware architecture of the low-cost BMS demonstration platform, showing the power path, sensing interfaces, STM32 controller, OLED display, and MOSFET load-control stage.}
\label{fig:hardware_architecture}
\end{figure}

The hardware design was implemented using a modular structure. The main functional blocks include the power supply, INA228 voltage and current measurement, NTC temperature sensing, OLED display, MOSFET load control, and STM32 interface connections, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:pcb_layout}. This modular arrangement makes the system easier to assemble, test, debug, and explain during demonstration. The NUCLEO-G491RE development board was selected as the main controller because it provides the required ADC, I\textsuperscript{2}C, GPIO, timer, Flash memory, expansion-header, and integrated STLINK-V3E debugging resources for embedded prototyping \cite{ref26}. The INA228 module was used for voltage and current monitoring because it integrates a 20-bit ADC, I\textsuperscript{2}C digital interface, bus-voltage and shunt-voltage measurement, and internal support for current, power, energy, and charge calculation \cite{ref5}. This reduces the need for a discrete analogue measurement front-end.

Temperature monitoring is implemented using an NTC thermistor divider connected to an STM32 ADC input. This provides a low-cost and compact method for demonstration-level thermal monitoring. The OLED display is connected through I\textsuperscript{2}C and provides local visual feedback of voltage, current, temperature, SoC, runtime information, and fault state. In addition to the electronic circuit, the platform includes a custom PCB, laser-cut mounting base, and 3D-printed battery cover to improve physical integration and visual clarity.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.62\textwidth]{figures/pcb_layout.png}
\caption{Schematic and PCB layout of the low-cost BMS demonstration platform, showing the main functional circuit blocks and their physical implementation.}
\label{fig:pcb_layout}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:software_flow} shows the software operation flow of the BMS demonstration platform. The embedded software was developed in C using STM32CubeIDE, with peripheral configuration supported by STM32CubeMX-generated initialisation code and firmware debugging/programming carried out through the onboard STLINK-V3E interface. The hardware circuit and PCB were designed and reviewed at schematic and layout level before assembly, so that the software development and hardware integration could be tested progressively rather than only at the final system stage. The firmware uses a bare-metal superloop rather than an RTOS, because the required tasks are lightweight and can be scheduled using simple timing logic. After power-on, the software initializes GPIO, I\textsuperscript{2}C, UART, TIM6, INA228, ADC, OLED, MOSFET control, and Flash-related data handling. The system then performs an initial preheating and OCV-based initialization stage of approximately 3~s before entering normal operation.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.36\textwidth]{figures/software_flow.png}
\caption{Software operation flow of the low-cost BMS demonstration platform, showing initialization, TIM6-based button handling, main-loop task execution, protection decision-making, and fault-state handling.}
\label{fig:software_flow}
\end{figure}

The software is organised using a two-layer timing structure. The first layer is a TIM6-based button sampling layer, which provides a fixed 5~ms time base for reading the onboard B1 button and performing debounce processing. The B1 user button is integrated on the Nucleo board and is connected to the STM32G4 microcontroller by default \cite{ref26}. This timing layer avoids relying only on the main loop for button detection, because OLED refresh and I\textsuperscript{2}C communication may introduce variable delays. The second layer is the main superloop, which handles sensor updates, state-machine processing, MOSFET control, runtime statistics, and OLED refresh.

During normal operation, the STM32 reads voltage and current from the INA228 through I\textsuperscript{2}C and reads temperature from the NTC circuit through the ADC. The control logic then evaluates over-temperature, over-current, under-current or load abnormality, and low-SoC warning conditions. Under abnormal conditions, the MOSFET can be turned off to place the load path in a safer state. If a severe fault is detected, the system enters a fault state, forces the MOSFET off, and waits for a hardware reset.

This hardware and software architecture keeps the platform simple, observable, and testable. It supports the main purpose of the project: demonstrating the sensing, processing, display, and protection chain of a low-cost BMS demonstration platform without introducing unnecessary industrial-level complexity.
\section{System-Level Design Rationale}

The system-level design was based on practical trade-offs between size, demonstrability, implementation risk, and functional completeness. Unlike a compact commercial BMS, the final platform was built on an approximately 30~cm $\times$ 30~cm mounting base. This larger layout was intentional, because the project is a demonstration platform rather than a miniaturised product. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:mounting_base}, the wider physical layout allows the battery pack, sensing module, controller, display, load path, and protection stage to remain visible and accessible. This improves debugging, inspection, and explanation during laboratory demonstration.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.36\textwidth]{figures/laser.png}
\caption{CAD model of the mounting base used to support the 30 cm × 30 cm demonstration layout.}
\label{fig:mounting_base}
\end{figure}

A second trade-off was made between integration risk and development efficiency. The NUCLEO-G491RE board was selected because it provides the required ADC, I2C, GPIO, timer, Flash, and debugging resources in a ready-to-use STM32 development platform~\cite{ref6}. Previous experience with STM32 development also reduced the learning time. Similarly, the INA228 module was selected because it integrates voltage, current, power, energy, and charge monitoring with an I2C interface~\cite{ref5}. Compared with a discrete analogue front-end, this reduced circuit design risk and allowed more time to be spent on system integration and testing.

The sensing and software choices also reflect the same design logic. An NTC thermistor was used for temperature sensing because it is small, low-cost, and sufficient for demonstration-level thermal monitoring. Thermocouples and platinum resistance sensors can provide higher performance in some applications, but their cost and interface complexity are less suitable for this platform. The firmware was implemented as a bare-metal superloop rather than an RTOS, because the required tasks are lightweight and can be handled using simple timing logic. A TIM6-based timing layer was used for button sampling and debounce, while the main loop handles sensing, display, protection, and runtime statistics.

More complex BMS functions, such as cell balancing and charging control, were not implemented because they would require additional hardware, more development time, and extra safety validation. These functions are important in complete industrial BMS designs~\cite{ref2}, but they are beyond the central purpose of this platform. SoC estimation was retained as a software extension feature because it improves battery-state representation and demonstrates that higher-level functions can be added on top of the core sensing chain~\cite{ref14}.
\Needspace{4\baselineskip}
Overall, the design prioritises visibility, testability, and clear functional demonstration over miniaturisation and industrial completeness. It is not intended to replace a commercial BMS, but it provides a practical and understandable demonstration of the core monitoring, display, and protection chain.


\chapter{Subsystem Design and Test}

\section{Voltage and Current Measurement}

Voltage and current measurement form the main electrical sensing function of the low-cost BMS demonstration platform. In this system, voltage is used to observe the battery pack condition and support basic protection decisions, while current is required to understand load behaviour, calculate power, and provide input for later state-of-charge estimation. Therefore, this subsystem acts as one of the most important information sources for the whole system.

The measurement subsystem uses a Texas Instruments INA228 digital power monitor, implemented through the Adafruit INA228 I\textsuperscript{2}C Power Monitor breakout board. This distinction is important because the INA228 itself is the measurement integrated circuit, while the Adafruit breakout board provides the practical module form and includes the 0.015~$\Omega$ shunt resistor used in this project \cite{ref5},\cite{ref28}. The INA228 was selected mainly because it provides high measurement precision while keeping the hardware implementation simple. According to the datasheet, the device integrates a 20-bit delta-sigma ADC, supports bus-voltage measurement up to 85~V, and provides maximum shunt-voltage and bus-voltage gain errors of $\pm 0.05\%$ \cite{ref5}. It can also report bus voltage, shunt voltage, current, power, energy, and charge through an I\textsuperscript{2}C interface \cite{ref5}. These features make it suitable for this demonstration platform because the project requires reliable voltage and current values, but does not require a complex industrial BMS analogue front-end.

In this project, the INA228 is connected in a high-side measurement configuration. The battery pack output passes through the INA228 measurement path before supplying the load and the MOSFET-controlled output stage. This allows the system to measure the pack voltage and discharge current without disturbing the system ground reference. From an implementation point of view, this was more practical than designing a custom current-sensing PCB using an operational amplifier, external ADC input, and discrete analogue filtering. The Adafruit breakout board also simplified the build process because it could be connected directly to the microcontroller and power path without additional precision analogue soldering work \cite{ref28}.

The INA228 communicates with the STM32G491RE microcontroller through I\textsuperscript{2}C. In practical wiring, only four main signal and power connections are needed: supply, ground, SCL, and SDA. This makes the subsystem easy to connect, inspect, and explain during demonstration. This is important for the aim of the project, because the platform is designed not only to function, but also to make the sensing and protection process physically clear and understandable. The use of a digital power monitor therefore supports both the technical reliability and the presentation clarity of the final system.

The raw digital readings from the INA228 are converted into physical voltage and current values in firmware. The bus voltage is calculated as
\begin{equation}
V_{BUS}=N_{BUS}\times 195.3125~\mu\mathrm{V}
\end{equation}
where $N_{BUS}$ is the raw bus-voltage register value. The shunt voltage is calculated as
\begin{equation}
V_{SHUNT}=N_{SHUNT}\times 78.125~\mathrm{nV}
\end{equation}
where $N_{SHUNT}$ is the raw shunt-voltage register value in the high-sensitivity shunt range. The load current is then obtained using Ohm's law:
\begin{equation}
I=\frac{V_{SHUNT}}{R_{SHUNT}}
\end{equation}
where $R_{SHUNT}=0.015~\Omega$ for the Adafruit INA228 breakout board used in this project \cite{ref5},\cite{ref28}. These equations provide a clear conversion path from the INA228 register values to the voltage and current values used by the firmware.

During testing, the measured battery pack voltage was typically around 11.1~V, which is consistent with the nominal voltage of the 3S lithium-ion battery pack used in the platform. The load current was usually in the range of several hundred milliamps, although the exact value changed with the load condition and battery state. The INA228 readings were repeatedly compared with a handheld multimeter, and the measured voltage and current values were found to match closely under normal test conditions. This confirmed that the subsystem was accurate enough for real-time OLED display, protection threshold checking, power-related calculation, and later SoC estimation.

The main limitation of this subsystem is that the final measurement stability still depends on practical implementation factors, such as wiring quality, shunt connection resistance, I\textsuperscript{2}C communication reliability, and electrical noise from the load path. Although the INA228 provides high-resolution digital measurement, this project did not include a detailed noise-characterisation experiment or a dedicated external analogue filtering stage. Therefore, the subsystem should be understood as a reliable and low-cost measurement solution for a demonstration platform, rather than a fully calibrated industrial BMS measurement front-end. The practical output of the voltage and current measurement subsystem is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:volt-current-display}. The OLED pages display a battery-pack voltage of 11.148~V and a load current of 173.3~mA, showing that the INA228 readings were successfully acquired by the STM32 and transferred to the user interface during operation.

