\section{Temperature Measurement}

Temperature measurement is an important function in the proposed low-cost BMS demonstration platform. Before integrating the NTC thermistor into the final sensing circuit, its resistance--temperature characteristic was first checked against the calculated Beta-model curve. This was done to confirm that the selected thermistor followed the expected nonlinear negative-temperature-coefficient behaviour and that the firmware conversion method was based on a reasonable sensing model.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.68\textwidth]{figures/ntc_resistance_temperature_curve.png}
    \caption{Comparison between measured NTC resistance values and the calculated resistance--temperature curve based on the Beta model.}
    \label{fig:ntc-rt-comparison}
\end{figure}

As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:ntc-rt-comparison}, the measured resistance values follow the same decreasing trend as the calculated curve. The resistance changes rapidly at lower temperatures and more gradually at higher temperatures, which is consistent with the expected behaviour of an NTC thermistor. The close agreement between the measured and calculated values indicates that the selected NTC thermistor and the Beta-model conversion are suitable for demonstration-level temperature monitoring in this project \cite{ref30}.

Battery temperature affects operating safety, measurement reliability, and later protection behaviour, so temperature monitoring is commonly treated as one of the key sensing requirements in battery management systems \cite{ref1}, \cite{ref3}, \cite{ref12}. In this project, the aim was not to implement a complex industrial thermal-management system, but to provide a simple, low-cost, and sufficiently accurate temperature-sensing method that could be integrated into the monitoring and display framework. Therefore, the NTC-based subsystem was selected as a practical solution for observing thermal behaviour and supporting the later MOSFET-based protection response.

The temperature measurement subsystem uses a TDK B57863S0103G040 NTC thermistor. This device has a nominal resistance of 10~k$\Omega$ at 25$^{\circ}$C and a Beta value of $B_{25/100}=3988$~K \cite{ref30}. It was selected because it is inexpensive, easy to obtain, and simple to integrate using a basic resistor-divider circuit. For a demonstration platform, this provides a practical balance between cost, implementation complexity, and measurement performance.

The practical temperature-sensing circuit is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:ntc-divider}. A fixed 27~k$\Omega$ resistor is connected between the 3.3~V supply and the ADC sensing node, while the NTC thermistor is connected between the same node and ground. The sensing node is connected to PA0, which is configured as an ADC input on the STM32G491RE. The STM32G491RE provides on-chip ADC resources suitable for embedded analogue sensing, while ADC accuracy can be affected by reference voltage, source impedance, sampling time, and noise conditions \cite{ref6},\cite{ref7}. A 0.1~$\mu$F capacitor is also connected between the sensing node and ground to reduce high-frequency noise and improve the stability of the sampled voltage.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.52\textwidth]{figures/ntc_divider_circuit.png}
\caption{NTC-based temperature measurement circuit connected to the STM32 ADC input.}
\label{fig:ntc-divider}
\end{figure}

The ADC conversion first converts the raw digital code into the sampled node voltage:
\begin{equation}
V_{NTC}=\frac{N_{ADC}}{4095}\times V_{DDA}
\end{equation}
where $N_{ADC}$ is the 12-bit ADC output code and $V_{DDA}$ is the ADC reference voltage, taken as 3.3~V in this project. The NTC resistance is then calculated from the voltage-divider relationship:
\begin{equation}
R_{NTC}=R_{FIX}\times \frac{V_{NTC}}{V_{DDA}-V_{NTC}}
\end{equation}
where $R_{FIX}=27$~k$\Omega$ is the fixed resistor. After the thermistor resistance is obtained, the temperature is estimated using the Beta model:
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{T}=\frac{1}{T_0}+\frac{1}{B}\ln\left(\frac{R_{NTC}}{R_0}\right)
\end{equation}
where $T$ is the absolute temperature in kelvin, $T_0=298.15$~K corresponds to 25$^{\circ}$C, $R_0=10$~k$\Omega$, and $B=3988$~K \cite{ref30}. Finally, the result is converted into degrees Celsius:
\begin{equation}
T_C=T-273.15
\end{equation}

The calculated temperature value is updated in firmware and displayed on the OLED interface together with the other system parameters. Fig.~\ref{fig:temp-display} shows the practical temperature display during testing. The displayed value behaved as expected under normal operating conditions, confirming that the NTC divider, ADC sampling, firmware conversion, and OLED display path were successfully integrated.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.35\textwidth]{figures/temp_display.jpg}
\caption{OLED display showing the measured temperature obtained from the NTC sensing circuit during testing.}
\label{fig:temp-display}
\end{figure}

The temperature-related protection threshold is implemented in firmware and can be adjusted according to the test requirement. However, the control behaviour is not discussed in detail in this subsection, because the MOSFET-based protection action is covered separately in Section~4.4. In this subsection, the focus is on the sensing chain itself: the NTC converts temperature variation into resistance change, the resistor divider converts this resistance change into voltage, and the STM32 ADC converts the voltage into a digital value for firmware processing.

The main limitation of this subsystem is that the final temperature accuracy depends on the tolerance of the NTC thermistor, the fixed resistor, and the ADC reference voltage. The measured value can also be affected by electrical noise, sensor placement, and thermal contact between the NTC and the monitored battery region. In addition, this project did not include a full multi-point temperature calibration procedure. Therefore, the measured temperature should be understood as sufficiently accurate for demonstration and basic protection purposes, rather than as a fully calibrated industrial temperature measurement.

\section{OLED Display and User Interface}

The OLED display was implemented to make the operating state of the BMS demonstration platform directly visible during testing. It provides local feedback for measured values, warning states, runtime information, and user interaction without requiring an external computer or serial terminal.

The display module is a 128$\times$64 monochrome OLED based on the SSD1306 controller. The SSD1306 integrates the display RAM, segment driver, common driver, and controller functions required for compact OLED operation \cite{ref29}. In this system, the OLED is connected to the STM32G491RE through I\textsuperscript{2}C3, so only four main connections are required: power, ground, SCL, and SDA. This keeps the wiring simple and makes the display module easy to test or replace.

Because the 128$\times$64 display area is limited, the interface was designed as a page-based user interface rather than a single crowded screen. The implemented interface includes eight main pages: SoC, SoC curve, temperature, voltage, current, runtime information, and two Flash-based statistics pages. This allows each parameter to be displayed with a clearer layout and larger text. The practical OLED interface is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:oled-display}.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.52\textwidth]{figures/UI.png}
\caption{OLED display pages used in the BMS demonstration platform for local presentation of key monitoring parameters.}
\label{fig:oled-display}
\end{figure}

User interaction is provided through the onboard B1 button on the NUCLEO-G491RE board. The B1 user button is integrated on the Nucleo board and is connected to the STM32G4 microcontroller by default \cite{ref26}. In the implemented firmware, a single press is used for page switching, while a double press is used for entering or saving editable settings where required. This provides a simple interaction method without adding an external keypad or rotary encoder.

The OLED interface links the main sensing and control functions of the platform. Voltage and current from the INA228, temperature from the NTC circuit, SoC estimation, runtime statistics, and fault-related information can all be presented locally. This makes the measurement, processing, display, and protection chain easier to observe during demonstration.

The main limitation is the small display size. Although 128$\times$64 pixels are sufficient for showing one or two key values per page, the screen cannot show all parameters, trends, warnings, and settings at the same time without reducing readability. The interface is also limited to local display and does not provide remote monitoring or long-term graphical data logging. However, for a low-cost BMS demonstration platform, the OLED provides a clear and practical local user interface.

\section{MOSFET-Based Protection Control}

The MOSFET-based protection control stage was implemented to convert software protection decisions into a physical load-disconnection action. In this project, the MOSFET is used as a controllable load switch rather than a high-frequency switching device. Under normal conditions, the load path is enabled. When an abnormal condition is detected, the STM32 changes the GPIO output state and turns the MOSFET off.

The protection stage uses an IRLZ44NPbF logic-level N-channel MOSFET. This device was selected because it can be driven by a microcontroller-level gate signal and has a low drain-source on-resistance, making it suitable for low-voltage load-switching applications \cite{ref32}. The implemented MOSFET control circuit is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:mosfet-schematic}. In the implemented system, the STM32 drives the MOSFET gate through a GPIO output, while the load current path is switched by the MOSFET stage.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.62\textwidth]{figures/mosfet_schematic.png}
\caption{MOSFET-based load switching circuit used for protection control in the BMS demonstration platform.}
\label{fig:mosfet-schematic}
\end{figure}

The MOSFET control decision is generated after the main sensing values have been updated. The firmware first reads the battery voltage and load current from the INA228, then reads the temperature from the NTC sensing circuit. These measured values are compared with configurable protection thresholds. The general protection decision can be expressed as
\begin{equation}
F_{prot}=F_T \lor F_I \lor F_L \lor F_{SOC}
\end{equation}
where $F_{prot}$ is the overall protection flag, $F_T$ is the over-temperature flag, $F_I$ is the over-current flag, $F_L$ is the load-abnormality flag, and $F_{SOC}$ is the low-SoC warning or severe low-state flag.

The temperature-related flag is defined as
\begin{equation}
F_T =
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
1, & T > T_{lim} \\
0, & T \leq T_{lim}
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $T$ is the measured temperature and $T_{lim}$ is the selected temperature threshold. In the implemented firmware, selected protection thresholds can be adjusted through the OLED user interface during testing. This allows different protection settings to be demonstrated without changing the hardware.

The current-related fault condition is evaluated using the measured load current:
\begin{equation}
F_I =
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
1, & |I| > I_{lim} \\
0, & |I| \leq I_{lim}
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $I$ is the measured current and $I_{lim}$ is the over-current threshold. A load-abnormality condition can also be detected when the load is expected to be connected but the measured current remains below a minimum level for a defined delay:
\begin{equation}
F_L =
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
1, & |I| < I_{min} \quad \mbox{for} \quad t > t_{delay} \\
0, & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $I_{min}$ is the minimum expected load current and $t_{delay}$ is the fault-confirmation delay. This delay avoids triggering a fault from a short transient measurement.

The GPIO output to the MOSFET is then determined by the protection flag:
\begin{equation}
GPIO_{MOS} =
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
0, & F_{prot}=1 \\
1, & F_{prot}=0
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
where $GPIO_{MOS}=1$ represents the normal enabled state and $GPIO_{MOS}=0$ represents the load-disconnection state. If a severe fault is detected, the firmware enters the fault state, forces the MOSFET off, and keeps the load disconnected until reset.

The subsystem was tested using a cause-and-effect method. First, the system was operated under normal load conditions to confirm that the MOSFET remained enabled. Then, abnormal conditions were introduced by heating the NTC thermistor or changing the external load so that the current exceeded the expected range. The OLED display and load behaviour were observed to confirm that the fault condition was detected and that the MOSFET output changed state. This verified the protection chain from sensing, threshold judgement, GPIO control, MOSFET switching, and physical load disconnection.

The main limitation of this protection stage is that the decision depends on sensor readings and firmware logic. Therefore, incorrect sensor data or unsuitable threshold settings can affect the protection response. Nevertheless, the implemented MOSFET control stage provides a clear and testable way to demonstrate how monitored battery conditions can be converted into a visible protection action.

\section{State-of-Charge Estimation}

State-of-Charge (SoC) estimation was implemented as a supporting function of the BMS demonstration platform. The purpose of this function is not to provide a highly calibrated battery model, but to give the user a reasonable indication of the remaining battery state on the OLED display. Since SoC cannot be measured directly by a single sensor, it must be estimated from measurable variables such as voltage, current, and previous operating history \cite{ref14,ref15}. In this project, a lightweight hybrid method was used, combining OCV-based initialisation, Coulomb counting, and a small voltage-based correction.

After power-on, the system performs an initialisation stage of approximately 3~s. During this period, the battery pack voltage is sampled several times using the INA228. Invalid readings outside the expected 3S battery range are rejected, and the remaining valid voltage readings are averaged. The average pack voltage is then divided by three to estimate the equivalent single-cell voltage. This voltage is used to initialise the SoC through an OCV lookup table. The OCV-based method is simple and suitable for initial estimation, although it is most reliable when the battery is close to a rested condition \cite{ref16,ref25}.

During normal operation, the SoC is updated mainly by Coulomb counting. The measured current from the INA228 is integrated over time using the actual sampling interval:
\begin{equation}
SOC_k = SOC_{k-1} - \frac{|I_k|\Delta t}{C_{nom}\times3600}
\end{equation}
where $SOC_k$ is the updated state of charge, $I_k$ is the measured load current, $\Delta t$ is the actual sampling interval in seconds, and $C_{nom}=2.2$~Ah is the nominal battery capacity used in this project. The use of the actual time interval rather than a fixed interval reduces integration error caused by small timing variations in the main loop.

To limit long-term drift, a lightweight OCV correction is applied when the load current is small. In this condition, the terminal voltage is less affected by load-induced voltage drop, so the OCV lookup result can be used as a weak correction term:
\begin{equation}
SOC = 0.98SOC_{cc}+0.02SOC_{ocv}
\end{equation}
where $SOC_{cc}$ is the Coulomb-counting result and $SOC_{ocv}$ is the SoC estimated from the voltage lookup table. A small 2\% correction factor was selected so that the displayed SoC can slowly move towards the voltage-based estimate without being dominated by short-term voltage fluctuation. The final SoC value is limited between 0 and 100\% before being displayed.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.15\textwidth]{figures/SOC.png}
\caption{OLED display showing the estimated SoC value using both numerical percentage and graphical bar representation.}
\label{fig:soc-display}
\end{figure}

The implemented SoC page is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:soc-display}. The OLED interface presents the estimated SoC both as a numerical percentage and as a graphical battery-style bar. The percentage gives a direct quantitative reading, while the bar provides a more intuitive visual indication of the remaining charge. In the wider software structure, the SoC result is also used as a supporting warning indicator, for example when the estimated value falls below the selected low-SoC threshold.

The main limitation of this method is its accuracy. Coulomb counting can accumulate drift when current measurement error, capacity uncertainty, or initial SoC error is present \cite{ref17}. The OCV correction also has limited accuracy under load because terminal voltage is affected by internal resistance, polarisation, temperature, and relaxation behaviour \cite{ref16}. More advanced methods such as Kalman-filter-based estimation or equivalent-circuit-model observers could improve dynamic SoC estimation \cite{ref14,ref18}. However, these methods require battery model parameters, calibration data, and more complex validation. For this project, they were not adopted because the aim was to keep the algorithm transparent, implementable on the STM32, and suitable for a low-cost demonstration platform.
\section{Runtime Statistics and Data Handling}


Runtime statistics were implemented to improve the observability and completeness of the BMS demonstration platform. In addition to real-time monitoring, the system records selected historical information so that previous operation, maximum values, and fault history can be inspected directly from the OLED interface. This function is mainly intended for demonstration and debugging rather than long-term industrial data logging.

The recorded data are divided into two OLED statistics pages. The first page presents general runtime information, including boot count, accumulated runtime, historical maximum temperature, and total output energy. The second page presents more detailed electrical and fault-related information, including maximum current, maximum power, total fault count, and the last recorded fault type. These values are derived from the existing sensing chain: voltage and current are obtained from the INA228, temperature is obtained from the NTC sensing circuit, and runtime is calculated from the firmware time base. Output power and accumulated energy are calculated from the measured voltage, current, and elapsed time.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.60\textwidth]{figures/flash_stats.png}
\caption{OLED statistics pages showing Flash-based runtime data retained by the BMS demonstration platform.}
\label{fig:flash-stats}
\end{figure}

The implemented statistics pages are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:flash-stats}. These pages allow the user to check not only the present operating state, but also the historical behaviour of the platform. This is useful during testing because it provides a simple way to confirm whether the system has experienced over-temperature, over-current, or other load-related abnormal conditions.

To retain the statistics after reset or power loss, the data are stored in the internal Flash memory of the STM32. A simple validity mechanism is used when loading the data: the firmware checks a magic number and checksum before accepting the stored structure. If the stored data are invalid, the statistics are reinitialised to default values. This prevents corrupted Flash contents from being treated as valid runtime records.

Flash writing is controlled using a dirty flag and a periodic save strategy. The statistics are not written continuously every time a value changes. Instead, the firmware marks the data as dirty when an update occurs and saves them at a longer interval, approximately once per minute during operation. This was selected because the platform is mainly used for short demonstration and laboratory tests, where second-by-second persistent logging is unnecessary. At the same time, periodic saving is sufficient to preserve useful runtime information while reducing unnecessary Flash erase/write cycles.

A clear function is also included for demonstration and maintenance. From the detailed statistics page, the user can enter a clear-confirmation process and reset the stored statistics when required. This makes the system easier to reuse between tests, while still showing that the platform can manage persistent runtime data rather than only displaying instantaneous measurements.

The main limitation of this data-handling method is that it is not a full data-logging system. The internal Flash memory has limited erase/write endurance, so it is not suitable for high-frequency logging. The stored values are summary statistics rather than complete time-series records, and the system does not include SD-card logging, PC-side data capture, or remote communication.
\section{Firmware Integration}

Firmware integration was carried out to connect the sensing, display, protection, SoC estimation, and runtime statistics functions into one operating system. The firmware was developed in C using STM32CubeIDE, which provides project configuration, code generation support, STM32 programming, and debugging through the ST-LINK interface \cite{ref31}. The NUCLEO-G491RE board also provides the required development and debugging resources, including accessible I/O headers and the integrated STLINK-V3E debugger/programmer \cite{ref26}.

The firmware uses a bare-metal superloop structure rather than an RTOS. This was selected because the required tasks are limited, periodic, and easy to schedule using software timing. After power-on, the firmware initialises the main peripherals, including GPIO, I\textsuperscript{2}C, UART, ADC, TIM6, INA228, OLED display, MOSFET control, and Flash-related data handling. The system then performs a short initialisation stage for voltage-based SoC estimation before entering normal operation.

The main loop is organised as a sequence of non-blocking module updates. Each module uses the current system tick to decide whether it should run, rather than using long blocking delays. The main tasks include button-event processing, sensor acquisition, protection control, user-interface state handling, display refresh, SoC update, and statistics update. This structure keeps the firmware readable and makes each function easier to test separately before full integration.

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Main firmware tasks and update periods used in the BMS demonstration platform.}
\label{tab:firmware-tasks}
\begin{tabular}{p{0.24\textwidth} p{0.14\textwidth} p{0.20\textwidth}}
\hline
\textbf{Task} & \textbf{Update method} & \textbf{Typical period} \\
\hline
B1 button sampling and debounce & TIM6 interrupt & 5~ms \\
Voltage, current, and temperature update & Main-loop polling & 200~ms \\
OLED display refresh & Main-loop polling & 250~ms \\
SoC history update & Main-loop polling & 5~s \\
TTE power averaging window & Circular buffer & 10~s \\
Flash statistics save & Dirty flag and periodic save & Approximately 1~min \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The button system is separated from the slower display and sensing tasks. TIM6 provides a fixed 5~ms timing base for button sampling and debounce processing, while the main loop converts the detected button edges into single-click or double-click events. This separation improves user-interface reliability because OLED refresh and I\textsuperscript{2}C transactions can introduce variable delays. The main loop then processes the resulting events through the user-interface state machine.

The firmware state handling includes normal display operation, editable setting pages, fault state, and statistics-clear confirmation. In normal operation, the system cycles through the OLED monitoring pages and continuously updates the measured values. In setting mode, selected thresholds can be adjusted before being saved. In fault state, the MOSFET is forced off and the OLED displays the fault information. This state-based structure prevents the display, setting operation, and protection response from interfering with each other.

Basic robustness measures were also included in the firmware. Sensor readings are checked before they are used in later calculations, and invalid values are prevented from directly affecting SoC estimation or protection decisions. Timing comparisons are based on tick differences rather than absolute tick values, which reduces the risk of errors when the system tick counter overflows. Flash statistics are saved using a dirty-flag strategy to avoid unnecessary erase and write operations.

Overall, the firmware integration provides the link between the individual subsystems described in the previous sections. The measurement modules provide voltage, current, and temperature data; the control module evaluates protection conditions; the OLED interface presents the system state; and the Flash module stores selected runtime information. This integrated software structure allows the platform to demonstrate a complete sensing, decision-making, display, and protection process in a compact embedded implementation.


\chapter{Overall System Test}


\section{Test Method and Setup}
The purpose of the overall system test was to verify that the completed platform could operate as an integrated BMS demonstration platform after full assembly. Unlike the subsystem tests in Chapter 4, this test focused on the complete operating chain, including sensing, display, user interaction, protection control, and Flash-based data retention. This is important because practical BMS operation depends on the correct integration of measurement, judgement, and protection response, rather than on isolated module performance alone [1]--[3].

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.35\textwidth]{figures/overall_system_test_procedure.png}
    \caption{Overall system test procedure used to validate the assembled BMS demonstration platform.}
    \label{fig:overall-test-procedure}
\end{figure}

As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:overall-test-procedure}, the test followed an incremental procedure from subsystem completion to final validation. The 3S battery pack was connected to the custom PCB, and the positive power path first passed through the INA228 high-side measurement stage before supplying the external lamp load. The return path then came back to the PCB and passed through the MOSFET switching stage. Therefore, the same physical current path was used for voltage/current measurement, OLED display, and protection validation [5], [26], [28].

The validation criteria were grouped into three levels. The first level checked basic operation, including successful power-up, OLED start-up, page switching, and valid voltage, current, and temperature readings. The second level checked integrated function, including sensor acquisition, display update, threshold evaluation, and MOSFET control. The third level checked consistency, where the displayed voltage and current were compared with multimeter readings, and the temperature response was checked under normal and heated NTC conditions.

Protection behaviour was tested by heating the NTC thermistor and changing the external load. During these tests, the OLED indication, lamp-load state, and MOSFET output were observed together. Flash retention was then checked by saving runtime statistics, resetting the platform, and confirming that the stored data could still be displayed. Overall, the test confirmed that the platform operated as a complete low-cost BMS demonstration platform rather than as a set of isolated subsystems.

\section{Integrated Functional Test}

The integrated functional test was carried out under normal operating conditions to confirm that the assembled platform could perform monitoring, display, interaction, load control, and data handling at the same time. The system was powered by the 3S battery pack and connected to the external lamp load through the INA228 measurement path and MOSFET switching stage. During the test, the OLED pages were checked while the system was running, and the B1 button was used to cycle through the main display pages.

