The results in Table~\ref{tab:integrated-functional-test} show that the main subsystems were able to operate together after full assembly. Voltage, current, and temperature values were updated on the OLED interface, the B1 button remained responsive, and the lamp load stayed connected when no fault condition was present. The Flash statistics page also remained accessible after reset, confirming that the data-retention function was integrated successfully. This test therefore verified the normal operating chain of the platform before the protection response was tested separately in Section~5.3.

\section{Protection Response Test}

The protection response test was carried out to verify that the platform could convert abnormal measurement conditions into a visible protection action. This test focused on the complete fault chain: sensor measurement, threshold judgement, OLED fault indication, and MOSFET-based load disconnection. This is a key requirement for a BMS, because monitoring data must be linked to protective action when unsafe or abnormal operating conditions are detected [1]--[3].

During the test, fault conditions were introduced in a controlled way. The NTC thermistor was heated to test the temperature-related protection response, and the external load condition was changed to test current-related abnormal behaviour. When the measured value exceeded the selected protection condition, the firmware entered the fault state, forced the MOSFET output off, and displayed the fault message on the OLED interface.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.22\textwidth]{figures/oled_fault_display.png}
    \caption{OLED fault display observed during the protection response test.}
    \label{fig:oled-fault-display}
\end{figure}

As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:oled-fault-display}, the OLED provided a clear local indication that the system had entered a fault condition. At the same time, the lamp load was disconnected through the MOSFET switching stage. This confirmed that the platform did not only detect abnormal measurements in software, but also produced a physical protection response through the load-control circuit. The use of a MOSFET as a controllable switching element is suitable for this demonstration-level protection function [32].

