\section{Summary of Test Results}

The overall system test confirmed that the assembled platform achieved the main functional objectives of the low-cost BMS demonstration platform. Under normal operating conditions, the system powered up correctly, acquired voltage, current, and temperature data, updated the OLED display, responded to B1 button input, and kept the external lamp load connected through the MOSFET stage. The Flash statistics function also remained accessible after reset, showing that the runtime data-retention function was successfully integrated.

The protection tests further showed that the platform could respond to abnormal operating conditions. Heating the NTC thermistor triggered the temperature-related protection response, while changing the load condition allowed the current-related protection behaviour to be checked. In these cases, the system displayed the fault condition on the OLED and disconnected the lamp load through the MOSFET stage. The low-SoC condition was also checked as a warning function, where the OLED provided a visible indication without being treated as the same type of forced shutdown fault.

Overall, the test results show that the final system operated as a complete sensing, display, judgement, protection, and data-retention chain. However, the validation was limited to short-duration laboratory functional testing. It did not include long-term reliability testing, certified safety assessment, full calibration over temperature, or operation across different battery ageing conditions. Therefore, the results demonstrate that the platform is suitable for educational demonstration and prototype-level validation, but they should not be interpreted as evidence of industrial-grade BMS compliance.

