\chapter{Discussion, Conclusion and Further Work}


\section{Key Project Outcomes}

The final system successfully passed both the individual subsystem tests and the overall integrated system test. This shows that the platform can operate as a complete and reliable BMS demonstration platform under the tested laboratory conditions. The key project outcomes can be summarised as follows.

\subsection*{1. Integrated Monitoring Function}

The platform successfully implemented the three core monitoring functions required for a basic BMS demonstration: voltage, current and temperature measurement. The INA228 was used for compact digital voltage and current measurement, avoiding the need for a complex discrete analogue front-end \cite{ref5}, \cite{ref28}. The NTC thermistor provided a low-cost temperature sensing method, and its measured resistance--temperature behaviour was shown to follow the expected calculated trend. These sensing functions formed the measurement basis for display, warning and protection decisions.

\subsection*{2. Observable User Interface and Runtime Information}

The OLED display allowed the measured values and system state to be shown locally without requiring an external computer. Voltage, current, temperature, SoC indication, fault information and runtime statistics could be viewed directly through the page-based interface. The B1 button also provided simple user interaction for page switching and setting access. In addition, the Flash-based runtime statistics allowed selected historical information to be retained after reset, improving the observability of the platform.

\subsection*{3. Functional Protection Response}

The MOSFET load-control stage successfully converted software protection decisions into a physical load-disconnection action. Under abnormal temperature, current or low-SoC conditions, the firmware could enter a fault state and disconnect the lamp load through the MOSFET. This demonstrated the complete protection chain from measurement, threshold judgement and OLED indication to physical output control.

\subsection*{4. Low-Cost and Expandable Demonstration Platform}

A major outcome of the project is the development of a low-cost and practical hardware platform. Inexpensive components such as the NTC thermistor, OLED display and MOSFET were integrated with the custom PCB to reduce wiring complexity. Mature modules such as the NUCLEO-G491RE and INA228 also reduced implementation risk. The NUCLEO-G491RE provides sufficient GPIO, ADC, I\textsuperscript{2}C, timer and Flash resources for the current system and future expansion \cite{ref26}. The transparent mounting base further improved the demonstration value by making the battery pack, PCB, controller, wiring and load path easy to observe during testing.

Overall, these outcomes show that the project achieved its main aim: to build a low-cost, observable and testable embedded BMS demonstration platform that links measurement, display, judgement, protection and runtime data handling in one complete system.

\section{Limitations and Trade-Offs}

Several trade-offs were made during the project. The final platform is larger and less compact than a commercial BMS, but this was acceptable because visibility and debugging access were more important for a demonstration platform. The transparent base and separated hardware layout made the system easier to inspect, explain and test, although this also means that the current platform is not designed as a miniaturised product.

The firmware also used a bare-metal superloop rather than an RTOS. This simplified the software implementation and was sufficient for the present monitoring and protection functions. However, OLED refresh and I\textsuperscript{2}C communication can sometimes affect the timing precision of B1 button response. Therefore, the current firmware structure is suitable for this demonstration platform, but it is less scalable than a task-based real-time design.

The project scope was also deliberately limited. Cell balancing, charging control and certified safety protection were not implemented because they would require extra hardware and longer validation. The SoC function was included mainly as a supporting display and state-indication feature, and its algorithm was kept lightweight rather than model-intensive. However, the low-SoC condition can still participate in the protection logic and trigger a forced shutdown fault when the selected threshold is reached.

The protection test was functional rather than long-term reliability testing. The system was tested under controlled laboratory conditions, but it was not tested under long-duration operation, vibration, enclosure constraints, or certified safety conditions. Therefore, the final system should be understood as a reliable educational and prototype-level BMS demonstration platform under tested conditions, not as an industrial BMS product.

\section{Conclusion and Future Work}

This project achieved its main aim by designing, building and testing a low-cost embedded BMS demonstration platform. The final system successfully demonstrated the core operating chain of a basic BMS, including voltage, current and temperature measurement, OLED-based status display, user interaction, MOSFET-based load protection, simplified SoC indication and Flash-based runtime statistics. The project therefore provides a clear and practical platform for demonstrating how sensing, judgement, display and protection can be linked in an embedded battery monitoring system.

The first area for future work is multi-cell and multi-sensor expansion. The present platform mainly demonstrates pack-level monitoring for a 3S battery pack. For a larger battery system, such as an automotive-style battery module, one voltage/current sensor and one temperature sensor would not be sufficient. Future development should therefore move towards cell-group-level or module-level monitoring, with multiple voltage, current and temperature sensing points distributed across the battery system. The available GPIO, ADC and I\textsuperscript{2}C resources of the STM32 platform provide a basis for this type of expansion.

The second improvement is the use of an RTOS-based firmware structure. As more functions are added, tasks such as sensing, display refresh, button handling, protection logic, data storage and communication should be separated more clearly. An RTOS would improve timing control, task priority management and software scalability, especially because OLED refresh and I\textsuperscript{2}C communication can affect the timing precision of user interaction in the current superloop design.

The third area is wireless or PC-based monitoring. At present, the OLED provides local display only. Future versions could add Bluetooth, USB serial logging or another communication interface so that voltage, current, temperature, SoC, fault state and runtime statistics can be displayed on a computer or mobile device. This would improve data visibility, make long-term testing easier, and allow the platform to act as a more useful laboratory monitoring tool.

The final area is the addition of cell balancing and charging control. These functions were not included in the present project because they require extra hardware and more detailed safety validation. However, they are important functions in a more complete BMS. Future work could add passive balancing circuits, cell-level over-voltage and under-voltage monitoring, charger enable/disable control and safer charge termination logic. This would move the platform from a monitoring and protection demonstrator towards a more complete BMS development platform.
